WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.122
You have a hundred billion play logs and want listens per user.

00:00:06.122 --> 00:00:10.243
Mickey's plays sit on machines four, twelve, seven hundred.

00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:14.364
How do you sum them without every machine talking to every other?

00:00:14.364 --> 00:00:18.486
Two primitives: distributed sort and distributed hash.

00:00:18.486 --> 00:00:24.142
Start with distributed sort.

00:00:24.142 --> 00:00:29.799
Phase one, every machine sorts its local data.

00:00:29.799 --> 00:00:35.455
Phase two, the cluster samples keys to find global split points, the boundaries that partition the output evenly.

00:00:35.455 --> 00:00:41.112
Phase three, every tuple moves to the machine that owns its split, and the splits concatenate into one globally sorted result.

00:00:41.112 --> 00:00:46.768
Big data frameworks use this for ORDER BY, for window functions, for anything that needs total order.

00:00:46.768 --> 00:00:51.749
Now the other primitive, hashing.

00:00:51.749 --> 00:00:56.730
Hash partitioning: hash each tuple's key, route it to the machine whose ID matches.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:01.710
Every play for one user lands on one machine, every play for another user on a different one.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:06.691
Each machine sees its full slice and aggregates locally, no peer talk.

00:01:06.691 --> 00:01:12.287
The payoff is a join.

00:01:12.287 --> 00:01:17.883
Matching rows must meet on one machine, so hash both tables on the join key.

00:01:17.883 --> 00:01:23.479
Take Listens and Users, joined on user id, and every row for one user id, from either table, lands on the same machine.

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:29.075
User seven's rows go to machine one, forty-two to two, eighty-eight to three.

00:01:29.075 --> 00:01:34.671
Each machine joins only its own slice, then unions the parts into the full result.

00:01:34.671 --> 00:01:40.267
No machine ever compares against another, so throughput scales with the cluster.

00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:42.828
But how do keys map to machines?

00:01:42.828 --> 00:01:45.389
The naive way is modulo: hash mod N.

00:01:45.389 --> 00:01:47.950
Change N and it breaks.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.510
Add one machine to three, three quarters of the data moves.

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:53.071
Add one to a hundred, ninety-nine percent moves.

00:01:53.071 --> 00:01:55.632
An hour of reshuffling.

00:01:55.632 --> 00:01:58.193
Modulo is brittle.

00:01:58.193 --> 00:02:02.040
Consistent hashing fixes it.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.886
Forget modulo, think in circles.

00:02:05.886 --> 00:02:09.733
The numbers from zero to two-to-the-thirty-second wrap into a ring.

00:02:09.733 --> 00:02:13.580
First, hash each machine and place the nodes on the ring.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:17.427
Next, hash each key and drop the keys onto the ring.

00:02:17.427 --> 00:02:21.274
Now the rule: walk clockwise from a key until you hit the first node, and that node owns it.

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:25.120
Here is the payoff.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.967
Add a machine, and it slots in at one spot.

00:02:28.967 --> 00:02:32.814
Only its arc of keys moves to the new node, the ones between it and its predecessor.

00:02:32.814 --> 00:02:36.661
Everybody else stays put.

00:02:36.661 --> 00:02:40.507
On average just one over N of the data moves, instead of three quarters with modulo.

00:02:40.507 --> 00:02:44.354
Cassandra, DynamoDB, and Memcached all use this idea.

00:02:44.354 --> 00:02:49.072
Sort and hash are the primitives behind MapReduce, Spark, and BigQuery.

00:02:49.072 --> 00:02:53.790
They hide hash partitioning behind GROUP BY and JOIN, sort behind ORDER BY.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:58.509
See the primitives, and you can predict how any query runs and where it stalls.

00:02:58.509 --> 00:03:03.227
That closes the distributed algorithms.

00:03:03.227 --> 00:03:07.945
Next, we turn to the real systems built on them, starting with Google's file system.
